e don\'t know if it\'s bridge at that point. So, we can do:\\n\\n var itemCount;\\n if (isBridge) {\\n    ... \\/\\/ we set itemCount in the bridge block\\n } else {\\n    itemCount = Number(quantity);\\n }\\n\\n Alternatively, after the if-else, we can set:\\n\\n if (!isBridge) {\\n    itemCount = Number(quantity);\\n }\\n\\n But the initial code sets `itemCount = quantity` and then in bridge condition, it recalculates. We can change the initial assignment to:\\n\\n var itemCount = 0;\\n\\n Then in non-bridge, we set it to Number(quantity), and in bridge, we set it to the reduce result.\\n\\n But that would change the structure.\\n\\n Given the complexity, and since we are already in a refactoring, let\'s restructure the code for clarity and robustness.\\n\\n Here\'s the final code with all the fixes:\\n\\n Steps:\\n 1. Use modelprice if available, else price for the price string in bridge condition.\\n 2. Handle the case when priceStr is null by defaulting to empty string.\\n 3. Convert quantity to a number in non-bridge condition for the product object and for itemCount.\\n 4. In the bridge condition, use safe array creation and mapping.\\n\\n Let me write the code:\\n\\n var currency = this.getAttribute(\'data-modelcurrency\') ?? (digitalData.page?.pageInfo?.currencyCode ?? \'TRY\');\\n var modelprice = this.getAttribute(\'data-modelprice\');\\n var price = this.getAttribute(\'data-price\');\\n var modelcode = this.getAttribute(\'data-modelcode\');\\n var quantityStr = this.getAttribute(\'data-modelqty\'); \\/\\/ We\'ll handle the default later\\n var modelname = this.getAttribute(\'data-modeldisplay\');\\n var category = this.getAttribute(\'data-pvisubtype\') ?? this.getAttribute(\'data-pvitype\');\\n\\n var bridgeAttr = this.getAttribute(\'an-la\');\\n var isBridge = (bridgeAttr == \\\"add-on:continue\\\" || bridgeAttr == \\\"add-on:go to cart\\\");\\n var products = [];\\n var totalPrice = 0;\\n var itemCount = 0;\\n var itemPrice = 0;\\n\\n \\/\\/ In non-bridge, we want to use the quantity from attribute or default to 1\\n var quantity = quantityStr ? Number(quantityStr) : 1;\\n\\n if (isBridge) {\\n    \\/\\/ For bridge, we need to split the strings\\n    var priceStr = modelprice || price || \'\';\\n    var modelcodeArray = modelcode ? modelcode.split(\',\') : [];\\n    var quantityArray = quantityStr ? quantityStr.split(\',\').map(v => Number(v.trim())) : [];\\n    var priceArray = priceStr ? priceStr.split(\',\').map(v => Number(v.trim())) : [];\\n    var modelnameArray = modelname ? modelname.split(\',\') : [];\\n    var categoryArray = category ? category.split(\',\') : [];\\n\\n    \\/\\/ Determine the maximum length to avoid undefined\\n    var maxLength = Math.max(modelcodeArray.length, quantityArray.length, priceArray.length, modelnameArray.length, categoryArray.length);\\n\\n    for (var i = 0; i < maxLength; i++) {\\n        products.push({\\n            id: modelcodeArray[i] ? modelcodeArray[i].trim() : null,\\n            category: categoryArray[i] ? categoryArray[i].trim() : null,\\n            name: modelnameArray[i] ? modelnameArray[i].trim() : null,\\n            quantity: quantityArray[i] || null,\\n            itemPrice: priceArray[i] || null,\\n        });\\n    }\\n\\n    totalPrice = priceArray.reduce((sum, val) => sum + (val || 0), 0);\\n    itemCount = quantityArray.reduce((sum, val) => sum + (val || 0), 0);\\n    itemPrice = totalPrice; \\/\\/ For the event value, we use totalPrice in bridge\\n } else {\\n    \\/\\/ In non-bridge, we have one product\\n    itemPrice = modelprice ? Number(modelprice) : Number(price || 0);\\n    products = [{\\n        id: modelcode,\\n        category: category,\\n        name: modelname,\\n        quantity: quantity,\\n        itemPrice: itemPrice,\\n    }];\\n    itemCount = quantity;\\n }\\n\\n rdt(\'track\', \'AddToCart\', {\\n    itemCount: itemCount,\\n    value: itemPrice, \\/\\/ In bridge, itemPrice is set to totalPrice, in non-bridge it\'s the item price\\n    currency: currency,\\n    products: products,\\n });\\n\\n However, note that in the non-bridge condition, we are using `itemPrice` which is set to either modelprice or price (converted to number). And in the bridge condition, we are setting `itemPrice` to `totalPrice` for the event value.\\n\\n This code is more robust and handles the cases where attributes might be missing.\\n\\n But note: the initial `itemCount` in non-bridge is now set to `quantity` (which is a number) and in bridge it\'s the reduce result.\\n\\n Also, we are using `quantityStr` to check for the attribute, then converting to a number for non-bridge. For bridge, we split `quantityStr` and convert each part.\\n\\n This should avoid the split error and handle missing attributes.\\n\\n Let me know if you have any questions.\\n\"}]', '2026-08-04 14:02:14', '2026-08-04 14:02:14'),
