cript is loaded.\\n    - If window.limy is not defined, it sets it to a function that pushes its arguments (the ones we want to track) into a queue (array) named `q` on the limy object.\\n\\n 3. e = i.createElement(y);\\n    - Creates a script element (since y=\'script\').\\n\\n 4. e.async = 1;\\n    - Sets the script to load asynchronously.\\n\\n 5. e.id = \'limy-analytics\';\\n    - Sets the id of the script element to \'limy-analytics\'.\\n\\n 6. e.src = \'https:\\/\\/sdk.getlimy.ai\\/p\\/limy-analytics.min.js\';\\n    - The source of the script.\\n\\n 7. e.setAttribute(m, g);\\n    - Sets an attribute on the script element. The attribute name is \'limy\' (m) and the value is the string g (the token).\\n    - So it becomes: <script ... limy=\\\"lmy_5lFFV95JlK9d0EIkx9rfdq0cKObaKJjG0PtoW8XJbUd\\\"><\\/script>\\n\\n 8. o = i.getElementsByTagName(y)[0];\\n    - Gets the first script element in the document.\\n\\n 9. o.parentNode.insertBefore(e, o);\\n    - Inserts the newly created script element (e) before the first existing script tag (o).\\n\\n The overall effect is that it loads the Limy Analytics script asynchronously and initializes a queue for any events that might be pushed before the script is fully loaded.\\n\\n Additionally, we are provided with a `digitalData` object that contains information about the page, user, and product. This data is typically used for analytics and tracking.\\n\\n How might we use this with Limy?\\n\\n We would likely want to send some of this `digitalData` to Limy. Since the script sets up a queue, we can push events to `window.limy` even before the script is loaded.\\n\\n Example:\\n\\n   window.limy(\'eventName\', { ...eventData });\\n\\n In our case, we might want to send the page view event with the digitalData.\\n\\n However, note that the provided script only sets up the loading and queue. The actual events would be sent by the loaded SDK.\\n\\n Let\'s consider sending a page view event:\\n\\n   window.limy(\'pageView\', {\\